.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B nysos
Start two interactive shells using SHELL, or /bin/sh, with an empty cue list.
.TP
.B nysos --demo
Load the built-in twelve-cue demo: Service fills the left column; Observer and Notes
are stacked in the right column. Most cues run commands without rearranging
panes. Only cues 5, 8, and 10 change layouts, hiding and restoring live shells.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-init demo.toml
Create a starter file without overwriting an existing file.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-config demo.toml \-\-check
Validate configuration without spawning shells or executing commands.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-config demo.toml
Start a saved demonstration. Playback begins manually; optional cue timers can advance it afterward.
.TP
.B nysos \-\-add-to-path \-\-install-completions
Configure PATH and completions for both Bash and Zsh, then exit.
.SH SHELL SETUP
The --add-to-path and --install-completions options accept bash, zsh, or all;
omitting the value selects all. They can be combined with each other, but not
with --config, --check, or --init. Open a new terminal after installation.
.PP
PATH setup uses the running executable's directory. It does not copy the binary.
Use --bin-dir DIR to select a stable directory containing nysos, especially with
versioned Nix store paths or profile symlinks. PATH entries are not duplicated.
.PP
Bash setup updates .bashrc and the first existing .bash_profile, .bash_login,
or .profile, creating .profile only if none exists. Zsh PATH setup updates
\&.zprofile and .zshrc under ZDOTDIR, or HOME when ZDOTDIR is unset. Completions
are loaded only for interactive shells; Zsh initializes compinit if needed.
.PP
Scripts are written under XDG_DATA_HOME/nysos/completions, falling back to
HOME/.local/share/nysos/completions: nysos.bash and _nysos. Rerun completion
installation after upgrading the binary.
.PP
Startup blocks are marked with nysos comments and updated in place. Existing
files get a .nysos.bak backup, which is never overwritten. Symlink targets are
updated while links are preserved. Each file replacement is atomic; the overall
multi-file installation is not. Output reports updated and unchanged files.
Remove the marked blocks and generated scripts to undo shell setup.
.SH INTERACTIVE CONTROLS
Press Ctrl-G (or the prefix selected with --prefix), release, then one of the
following keys. Supported prefixes are ctrl-g, ctrl-a, ctrl-b, and f12.
.TP
.B n, Enter
Send the next command to its target pane and advance.
.TP
.B s
Skip the next command.
.TP
.B e
Edit the current queue item. Applying rewinds that item without executing it.
.TP
.B o
Open the full demo editor to change the title, add or reorder cues, or edit panes. Ctrl-L loads a preview; Ctrl-S saves and
applies; Ctrl-G applies without saving; Esc cancels. F2, F3, and F4 are aliases.
Applying a full demo resets queue progress. Matching shell sessions are reused.
.TP
.B a
Add and focus an ad hoc shell pane, up to 16 panes total.
.TP
.B Tab, Right / Shift-Tab, Left
Focus the next / previous pane. Keys 1 through 9 select a pane directly.
.TP
.B 0, c
Show and focus the cue list, or toggle its visibility, respectively.
.TP
.B l, h
Cycle the layout or toggle the header, respectively.
.TP
.B x
Restart the focused shell, ending its current session.
.TP
.B ?, q
Show help or quit, respectively. Esc closes help.
.PP
In the cue list, Up/Down selects, Enter sends the selected cue, and e edits it.
Plain p previews the selected cue\(aqs next pending command over each pane.
Panes without pending commands say so. Up/Down scrolls long preview text; Esc
closes all previews. Previewing executes nothing and leaves progress unchanged.
Plain t types the first remaining command per target pane without sending Enter,
then focuses the first target pane. Edit and submit each line in its shell. Start
at empty shell prompts; input is inserted at the cursor. Control characters
(including tabs) are rejected. Progress is unchanged; skip manually submitted
commands or select a later cue to avoid sending them again.
Plain o opens the full demo editor to add cues or edit the demo title.
Home/End and Page Up/Down browse; the wheel scrolls the list. Click selects
without executing. Esc returns to the previous shell. Enter resumes the current
partially sent cue or replays an earlier cue. Commands are sent in order without
waiting for completion. Selection advances, staying on the last cue at the end;
Enter on the completed last cue replays it. Ctrl-G n still sends one command.
.PP
Without the prefix, normal input and Ctrl-C go to the focused shell.
Alt-Left/Right rotates focus. In the ghostty key profile Alt-B/F also rotates
focus. Ghostty on macOS sends Alt-B/F for
Option-Left/Right; these also become focus shortcuts instead of shell word
navigation. Alt-Up/Down is forwarded to the shell. Click to focus; drag shared borders to resize.
Grid row heights remain equal. Wheel events scroll history or reach applications
requesting mouse input; Shift-wheel requests history when forwarded by the host.
.PP
Cmd-click opens HTTP(S) links locally on macOS when the terminal forwards the
click. Alt-click is the portable fallback. Plain URLs spanning rows are not joined.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Demo files use TOML. No file is automatically discovered. The top-level fields
are title (nonempty text, default "nysos demo"), prefix (ctrl-g by default), header (boolean, default true), cue_list (boolean, default true),
cue_width (16 to 60 columns, default 26, capped at half the available width),
layout (columns, rows, grid, or a nested layout table), panes, and
queues. Omitted panes use the two interactive defaults; omitted queues are empty.
Use queues = [] for an interactive-only demo with custom panes.
The cue list is visible and focused at startup by default. Set cue_list = false
to start with it hidden and the first shell focused.
.PP
Each pane table has id (unique identity), optional title (display label), shell, args, optional cwd,
scheme (ocean, ember, forest, mono), and weight (1 to 1000). There must be 1 to 16
panes. The shell defaults to SHELL or /bin/sh; args defaults to an empty array;
id defaults to shell; title defaults to id; scheme defaults to ocean; weight defaults to 1.
.PP
Each queue may also specify layout, using the same presets or tree schema as
the global layout. It applies when executing or typing commands, not previewing
or skipping. Omission retains the active arrangement. Full-demo apply restores
the global layout. Resizing edits the active cue layout or, without an override,
the global layout. Save to persist these changes.
.PP
Each queue table requires a nonempty name and one or more commands, with at
most one command per pane within that queue. Repeated targets are rejected.
Use additional cues for separately triggered commands in the same pane. To run
multiple shell commands in one pane from a single cue, combine them into one
command entry with shell separators: cd /tmp && pwd runs pwd only if cd succeeds;
pwd; ls runs ls regardless of the first command's exit status in sh, Bash, and Zsh.
The header displays the total number of command entries, such as "3 commands".
Each queue has an
optional description. Each command requires pane (an existing pane ID) and
exactly one of command (nonempty single-line input, with no NUL, CR, or LF),
keys (key tables), or clear = true.
Optional command title and scheme change the target pane when sent or typed,
without changing its ID or restarting its shell. Overrides persist until changed;
preview and skip do not apply them. Titles must be nonempty without control characters.
Saving preserves initial pane settings and cue overrides; full-demo apply restores
initial appearance. Legacy pane name is an alias for id; do not specify both.
.PP
Paths are relative to the launch directory; configured paths do not expand
shell variables or tilde. The --check option validates structure and references,
not executable paths, directories, command syntax, or service availability.
.SS Nested layouts and resizing
A layout table is either a leaf with pane (stable pane ID), or a split with
required direction (columns or rows) and children (2 to 16 nodes). Either node
accepts weight (1 to 1000, default 1), relative to its parent. Root weight has no
sizing effect. Every configured pane must appear exactly once. Maximum nesting
depth is 16. Unknown fields and pane IDs are rejected. Pane-table weights apply
only to presets; tree weights apply to custom layouts.
.PP
Columns divide width; rows divide height. They may be nested in any combination.
The sidebar, header, and footer remain outside the tree. Saving persists node
weights. Ctrl-G l replaces a custom tree with columns. Ctrl-G a adds a pane to
the right, preserving the existing arrangement inside its group.
.PP
Drag a shared border to resize adjacent panes or nested groups. Font/window size
changes recompute the layout from terminal cell dimensions and resize child PTYs.
Stored proportions remain unchanged. Very small terminals may clip pane content.
.PP
Mouse input needs press, motion, and release forwarding from the terminal and any
multiplexers. For tmux use set-option -g mouse on; over SSH allocate a PTY with -t.
Support cannot be inferred from TERM alone. If dragging is unavailable, focus a
shell and use Ctrl-G then < or > for width, - or + for height. The nearest split
on that axis is resized. --no-mouse disables capture for the whole session.
See docs/guide/layouts.md for compatibility details and examples/nested-layout.toml
for a five-pane example.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SHELL
Default shell executable for panes that omit shell. The built-in and exported
twelve-cue example explicitly uses /bin/sh.
Falls back to /bin/sh when unavailable.
.TP
.B TERM, COLORTERM
Child PTYs receive xterm-256color and truecolor, respectively.
.TP
.B SSH_CONNECTION, SSH_TTY
If either is present, local macOS Command-key sampling is disabled.
.TP
.B HOME, ZDOTDIR, XDG_DATA_HOME
Control the user shell installation locations. HOME must be absolute.
A relative XDG_DATA_HOME is ignored; relative ZDOTDIR resolves from the launch
working directory.
.SH INSTALLATION
Homebrew: brew install mccodeman/tap/nysos. Source: clone the repository, then
nix develop --command make install; add the Cargo bin directory to PATH.
Release assets provide Linux amd64/arm64 Debian (.deb), RPM (.rpm), and portable
archives, plus macOS archives. The release install.sh downloads and verifies the
archive against SHA256SUMS, installing under HOME/.local without sudo.
Older releases such as v0.1.1 lack these binary assets. See
docs/guide/installation.md for package-manager commands, availability, and upgrades.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B demo.toml
Initial save-path suggestion when no configuration file is selected. Not loaded automatically.
.TP
.B docs/reference/configuration.md
Complete configuration reference in the source distribution.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including help, version, initialization, validation, or normal exit.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, file, PTY, or runtime failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH NOTES
Each advance sends one command plus Enter to the target pane's current foreground
program. Wait for a shell prompt; there is no completion detection or scheduler.
Changing a pane's id, shell, args, or cwd starts a replacement session.
Closing panes signals their shells; deliberately detached jobs may outlive them.
.PP
macOS is the primary platform, Linux secondary. Native Windows interaction is
deferred; use WSL. An interactive terminal is required on stdin and stdout,
except for help, version, --init, --check, and shell setup options.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR zsh (1)
.PP
The source distribution contains user guides under docs/guide/.
.SH BUILD INFORMATION
The --version and --version-full switches report the package version, UTC build
date/time, Git commit,
exact tags, Git description, compiler, target, and build profile. The -V switch
prints only the name and package version. Information is embedded at build time;
Git is not required at runtime. Unavailable metadata is shown as unknown.
Nix builds preserve the flake revision but generally lack tag metadata.

.SH TMUX AND SSH
Ctrl-G avoids the default tmux Ctrl-B prefix. Enable tmux mouse forwarding with
"tmux set-option -g mouse on". With --prefix ctrl-b under default tmux, press
Ctrl-B twice before each nysos command. nysos does not change tmux bindings.
.PP
Use ssh -t to allocate a remote terminal. Run nysos inside remote tmux to retain
sessions across disconnects. Prefix-Tab works when Alt-arrow keys are intercepted.
URL openers run on the remote host, not the SSH client. Native macOS Command-key
sampling is disabled over SSH and inside tmux; use client terminal link detection
for a client-side browser. Modal editor shortcuts are independent of the prefix.

.SH TERMINAL KEY PROFILES
The --terminal-keys option and terminal_keys TOML field accept auto (default),
standard, or ghostty. Auto detects Ghostty from TERM_PROGRAM=ghostty or, when
TERM_PROGRAM is absent or tmux, TERM=xterm-ghostty. Other terminal identities
use standard mappings, preserving Alt-B/F for the shell. Both profiles support
standard Alt-Left/Right. Use an explicit profile when tmux or SSH masks the client
terminal identity, or when terminal keybindings differ from their defaults.
Detection uses the launch environment; reconnecting tmux does not re-detect it.
The resolved profile appears in the help title.

.SH KEY ACTIONS AND AUTOMATIC PLAYBACK
Each commands entry must contain exactly one of command, keys, or clear = true.
For example: keys = [{ key = "Ctrl+C", repeat = 2 }]. Key tables require key;
repeat defaults to 1, with at most 4096 presses total per pane entry.
Names include Esc, Enter, Tab, BackTab, Backspace, Space, arrow/navigation keys,
Insert, Delete, F1-F12, and literal Unicode characters. Ctrl/Alt/Shift modifiers
are supported where terminal encoding exists; Command/Super is not supported.
The configuration reference lists every valid combination.
.PP
Preview labels keys and repeat counts separately from shell commands.
The t key sends key/clear actions immediately. Key/clear-only cues advance just
like Enter; mixed cues type commands without Enter, send other actions, and leave
progress unchanged. Enter afterward repeats every action.
Native clear sends no PTY input, preserves cursor position and normal history,
and does not stop the running process or erase its input buffer.
.PP
Each cue accepts advance_after_ms, an integer from 1 through 86400000.
After dispatch, its timer executes the next cue without waiting for completion.
Top-level loop = true wraps the playlist (default false). Startup never begins
playback automatically. A cue without a timer stops the automatic chain.
Space in Cues pauses/resumes an armed timer. Modals suspend the countdown;
browsing cues, restoring bookmarks, or applying configuration cancels it.
.SH OUTPUT BOOKMARKS
In Cues, s restores that cue's recorded effective layout and latest bookmarks
in visible panes and action targets; b restores the current live layout and
returns all panes to bottom. Browsing backward or forward preserves the live
layout and weights. Cue layouts may hide panes; their shells keep running and
collecting output. These are not undo or replay controls.
Bookmarks survive resizing, layout changes, gutter toggles, and text reflow.
They are in-memory and expire on history deletion or eviction from
its 10000-line history, alternate-screen changes, pane replacement, or config edits.
Unavailable bookmarks are reported; their recorded layout can still be restored.

.SH FEATURE GATES AND LINE NUMBERS
The line-numbers runtime gate defaults on, including in the built-in demo,
and requires no special build. If it causes problems, use
--disable-feature line-numbers or top-level features = []. Omitting features
uses the default list; an explicit array replaces it. --enable-feature can
re-enable a gate. --disable-feature wins over TOML and CLI enables.
Both options are repeatable; unknown names are rejected. Startup overrides are
saved in the editable demo; later full-demo edits can change the gate list.
.PP
Top-level line_numbers defaults false: gutters start hidden, including in the
built-in demo. The enabled gate makes them available to turn on during a session.
Visibility settings have no effect without the gate.
Optional pane and cue-action line_numbers booleans override visibility.
In a focused shell, prefix then # toggles its gutter without restarting it;
save the full demo to persist that pane default. Cue overrides can change it again.
.PP
The attached eight-column gutter shares terminal scrollback and is excluded from
PTY width, input, and output. Wrapped logical lines show a continuation marker.
Gutter clicks focus; wheel events scroll history. Cursor and application mouse
coordinates exclude the gutter. Host-terminal selection may still copy it.
Narrow panes collapse the gutter; alternate-screen applications leave it blank
with its width reserved. Visibility changes resize the PTY; bookmarks and retained line numbers follow reflow.
.PP
Numbering reflects the current buffer, not a permanent transcript. History clear,
shell restart, gate re-enable, or unsupported buffer rearrangement restarts numbering. Layout changes preserve
retained line numbers, including full-history and alternate-screen reflow. Values above 999999 display ++++++.
See docs/guide/feature-gates.md for full behavior and experimental limitations.
